The real killer came next. Soot and dust spread around the world and dimmed the Sun for months, maybe years. Plants struggled, and the [[foodchain|food chain]] fell apart from the bottom up.

In the oceans, tiny [[plankton|plankton]] that feed everything else crashed. [[@ammonites|Ammonites]], which had lived for over 300 million years, died out. So did the great [[@ocean-reptiles|sea reptiles]], the mosasaurs and [[@marine-reptiles-plesiosaur|plesiosaurs]]. On land, no animal much bigger than a medium-sized dog is thought to have survived.

{{story:asteroid-size}}

Insects have been carrying pollen for more than 100 million years. The [[@united-nations|UN's]] food agency estimates that about three out of four kinds of leading food crops do better with animal [[@pollinators|pollinators]], mostly insects.

Some depend on them almost completely. Mango flowers are visited by flies, bees and wasps. Cacao trees, which give us [[@chocolate-making|chocolate]], are pollinated by tiny midges. Cotton, mustard and many pulses grow better with insect visits too. Wild flowers would suffer even more, and with them the animals that eat their seeds and fruits.

{{story:pollinators}}

The scientists, led by Marie Dacke from Sweden, worked with the African beetle *Scarabaeus satyrus*. They noticed that the beetles rolled straight even on dark nights with no Moon. So they took them into the Johannesburg Planetarium.

When the dome showed the full starry sky, the beetles rolled straight. When it showed only the Milky Way, they still rolled straight. When the scientists put little cardboard caps on the beetles' heads, the beetles were lost. Their eyes can't see individual stars, but the glowing stripe of the [[@milky-way|Milky Way]] is enough.

{{story:planetariums}}

Final assembly is like a slow [[@made-cars|car factory]]. On some lines, planes inch forward on a moving floor, a few centimetres a minute. Workers climb ladders and scaffolds to fit miles of wiring, hydraulic pipes, windows and doors.

Boeing's factory at Everett, near Seattle, is the largest building in the world by volume. It covers about 40 hectares, and Disneyland's main park could fit inside it. It was first built in the 1960s for the 747, and each of its biggest doors is about as wide as an American football field is long.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

लियोनार्दो ने उसके होंठों और आँखों के कोनों को बिना किसी तीखी रेखा के छाया में घोल दिया। इस तरीके को *स्फ़ुमातो* कहते हैं।

देखने की प्रक्रिया पर शोध करने वाले वैज्ञानिकों के पास इसका एक जवाब है कि उसकी मुस्कान आती-जाती क्यों लगती है। तुम्हारी आँख की [[retina|रेटिना]] का बीच का हिस्सा बारीक चीज़ें देखने में माहिर है, जबकि नज़र के किनारे हल्की परछाइयाँ बेहतर पकड़ते हैं। जब तुम सीधे उसके मुँह को देखते हो, तो मुस्कान छोटी लगती है। जब तुम उसकी आँखों को देखते हो, तो मुँह के आसपास की परछाइयाँ मुस्कान को बड़ा दिखाती हैं।

{{story:renaissance}}

Confucius never got the high government job he wanted, at least not for long. He travelled from state to state for years, looking for a ruler who would follow his advice, and found none.

But he became famous as a teacher. Unusually for his time, he said he would teach anyone who brought even a small gift, whether rich or poor. Tradition says he had thousands of students. His teaching was mostly conversation, and the Analects is full of short exchanges in which a student asks a question and the Master answers.

{{story:chinese-characters}}

In China, keeping crickets goes back more than a thousand years. Owners kept them in small boxes or hollow gourds, sometimes carved with pictures, and carried them close to the body in winter to keep them warm. Some prized the insects' chirping. Others trained males to fight.

Goldfish come from China too. Wild carp sometimes have a gold-coloured fish among them, and Chinese breeders began keeping those as pets over a thousand years ago. Today, Hong Kong has a street nicknamed Goldfish Market, where fish hang in plastic bags along shop walls like bunches of coloured balloons.

{{story:parrots-talk}}

ज़्यादातर टुकड़े तुम्हारे कपड़ों, बिस्तर, कुर्सी और फ़र्श पर गिरते हैं। कुछ नहाते समय बह जाते हैं। कुछ हवा में तैरते रहते हैं, इसीलिए कभी-कभी धूप में चमकते दिख जाते हैं।

बहुत-से जीव इससे खुश रहते हैं। सूक्ष्म [[@gross-dust-mites|धूल के कीट]], जो मकड़ियों के रिश्तेदार हैं, बिस्तरों, तकियों और सोफ़ों में रहते हैं और त्वचा के टुकड़े खाते हैं। एक इस्तेमाल किए गद्दे में इनके हज़ारों जीव रह सकते हैं। ज़्यादातर लोगों के लिए ये बेकसूर हैं, हालाँकि इनकी बीट एलर्जी भड़का सकती है।

{{story:gross-dust-mites}}

ऊँचा गाने के लिए स्वरयंत्र की नन्ही मांसपेशियाँ स्वर रज्जुओं को खींचकर पतला और कसा हुआ कर देती हैं, जैसे [[@guitar|गिटार]] या सितार का तार कसना। नीचा सुर लगाने के लिए ये ढीले और मोटे हो जाते हैं। ज़्यादा हवा धकेलने से आवाज़ तेज़ हो जाती है।

सधे हुए [[@singing|गायक]] इन मांसपेशियों और अपनी साँस पर बहुत बारीकी से काबू रखना सीखते हैं। कुछ [[@opera|ऑपेरा]] गायक ऐसा सुर लगा सकते हैं जिससे काँच का पतला गिलास इतनी ज़ोर से थरथराए कि चटक जाए, हालाँकि इसके लिए तेज़ और बिल्कुल सही [[pitch|पिच]] वाला सुर चाहिए।

{{story:singing}}

Two of the most common jobs for algorithms are searching and sorting. Suppose you're looking for "mango" in a dictionary. You wouldn't start at page one. You'd open it near the middle, see whether "mango" comes before or after, and keep halving. That halving method is called [[@binary-code|binary]] search, and it can find one word among a million in about 20 steps.

Sorting has many algorithms. One compares neighbours and swaps them, over and over. It's simple but slow for big lists. Others split the list into small piles, sort each, and merge them back. For a million items, the clever methods can be thousands of times faster.

{{story:lunchbox-dabbawala}}

Building the hardware was hard, but making the shows was harder. Producers had to talk to a farmer in Odisha, a weaver in Rajasthan and a schoolchild in Karnataka, each in their own language. Teams travelled to villages to film real people instead of actors in city studios.

Keeping the sets running was its own job. A village TV could break, run out of power or get damaged by [[monsoon|monsoon]] damp, so technicians from nearby repair centres did regular rounds. Researchers visited too, asking villagers what they understood, what they enjoyed and what bored them.

{{story:satellites-help}}

The emperor Justinian, who ruled from 527 to 565, tried to win back the lost western lands, and his generals retook Italy and North Africa for a time. He also had Rome's laws collected and organised into one great code. Justinian's Code later shaped the legal systems of many European countries.

Justinian's most famous building is Hagia Sophia, "Holy Wisdom", completed in 537. Its dome, over 30 metres across, seems to float above a ring of windows. For centuries it was the largest church in the Christian world. It later became a mosque, then a museum, and since 2020 it has been a mosque again.

{{story:silk}}

In British India, women campaigned from 1917, with [[@sarojini-naidu|Sarojini Naidu]] among those who spoke for them. From 1921 some provinces, starting with Madras, let a small number of women vote, usually those who owned property or had education.

When India wrote its [[@constitution|Constitution]], its makers chose universal adult franchise: every citizen aged 21 or over could vote, whatever their sex, religion, caste, wealth or education. Many people abroad doubted it would work in a country where most people could not read. In the first general election of 1951–52, [[@election-commission|election officials]] used picture symbols for parties and separate ballot boxes for each candidate.

{{story:vote-how}}

The kitchens run nearly all day and night. Huge iron cauldrons, some big enough to sit inside, bubble with dal and kheer. Machines help with some jobs, such as flipping [[@roti-chapati|rotis]], but a great deal is still done by hand. Volunteers sit together chopping onions, rolling dough and stirring pots with paddles like oars.

Guests sit in long rows on mats in a big hall. Servers walk along with buckets and ladles, filling metal plates and topping them up. When one sitting finishes, the hall is cleared and washed, and the next group comes in. Another team scrubs thousands of plates and passes them back. On festival days, the number of meals can pass 1 lakh.

{{story:volunteering}}

Atlanta's airport tops the list almost every year. That's not because Atlanta is huge. It's because one of America's biggest airlines uses it as a hub, a giant meeting point where passengers change planes. The airport says about 80 percent of Americans live within a two-hour flight.

Dubai is second, with about 95 million passengers, and it's the busiest airport for international travellers. Planes land and take off there day and night, linking Europe, Africa, Asia and Australia. Keeping them apart is the job of [[@air-traffic|air traffic]] controllers, who guide each one in and out.

{{story:air-traffic}}

Ice is lighter than water, so when a river freezes, the ice forms on top, like a lid. That lid keeps the cold air off the water underneath, so the river keeps flowing below. Explaining why [[@why-ice-floats|ice floats]] is one of the odd tricks of water. Most liquids get heavier when they turn solid.

So walkers on the chadar are often standing on ice with water rushing beneath it. The ice can be glassy blue, snowy white or covered in a film of flowing water. Guides tap it with sticks and listen. A dull thud or a hollow ring can mean very different things.

{{story:why-ice-floats}}

In 1978 she married N. R. Narayana Murthy. In 1981 he and six friends started a software company called Infosys, and she lent him ₹10,000 from her savings to help. Infosys grew into one of India's biggest companies, part of the story of how [[@indian-startups|Indian startups]] can become giants.

In 1996 she became head of the Infosys Foundation. Under her, it built tens of thousands of school library collections, supported hospitals and helped people hit by floods and other disasters. She retired from the foundation at the end of 2021. Before all this, she had also taught computer science to college students in Bengaluru for many years, and she has said that teaching was one of the jobs she loved most.

{{story:indian-startups}}

In southern China and Southeast Asia, people have long made sauces by fermenting fish with salt. In the Hokkien language, one of these was called something like *kê-tsiap*. British traders in the 1600s tasted it and liked how it kept for months without spoiling.

Back in England, cooks tried to copy it. They didn't have the right fish, so they used what was around them: mushrooms, walnuts, oysters and anchovies. A 1700s English ketchup was dark, thin and salty. Cookbooks of the time often had several ketchup recipes side by side. You can still buy mushroom ketchup in Britain today.

{{story:fermentation}}

Your [[@tongue-taste|tongue]] has a few thousand [[@taste-smell-link|taste buds]], each holding a cluster of sensing cells. They pick up five basic tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter and [[@food-science-umami|umami]]. Umami is the savoury taste of tomatoes, mushrooms, cheese and soups. A Japanese chemist named Kikunae Ikeda named it in 1908 after studying a [[@kelp-forests|seaweed broth]].

But taste is only a small part of flavour. Smells from food drift up the back of your throat into your [[@nose-smell|nose]], which can tell apart a vast number of different smells. When you have a blocked nose, food tastes dull for exactly this reason.

{{story:nose-smell}}
